<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Windsor, Connecticut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1633">1633</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Windsor is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> north of Hartford, founded in 1633 as a trading post of Plymouth Colony and settled by colonists from Massachusetts, making it one of the oldest English settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>. With Hartford and Wethersfield it formed the Connecticut Colony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/windsor-connecticut.jpg" alt="Windsor, Connecticut" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Phelan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Windsor_Town_Hall,_Windsor_CT.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Windsor lies in Hartford County on the northern border of Hartford. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> forms its eastern boundary and the Farmington River joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> within the town. It lies about 50 miles (80 km) up the river from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-island-sound">Long Island Sound</a>, at the head of waters navigable by ship and above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fort at Hartford, a position that gave the English an advantage in trading with the Native peoples before they reached the Dutch. The Native peoples called the area Matianuck.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the first part of the 17th century the Pequot and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohegan">Mohegan</a> were at war, and the Podunk were forced to pay tribute to the more powerful Pequot, who claimed their land. The Podunk invited a small party of settlers from Plymouth to settle as a mediating force, granting them land at the confluence of the Farmington and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> rivers. After Edward Winslow came from Plymouth to inspect it, William Holmes led a party that arrived on 26 September 1633 and founded a trading post.</p><p>Other settlers followed. In 1635 a party of about 30, sponsored by Sir Richard Saltonstall and led by the Stiles brothers, settled in the area, and migrants from Dorchester, Massachusetts, led by Roger Ludlow, outnumbered and soon displaced the Plymouth group, who returned to Plymouth in 1638 after selling their parcel. On 21 February 1637 the colony's General Court renamed the settlement Windsor, after Windsor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>, and incorporated it as a town with Hartford and Wethersfield. Many later towns, among them Simsbury, Suffield and Windsor Locks, were formed from its original boundaries.</p><p>The first highway in the Connecticut Colony opened in 1638 between Windsor and Hartford and was extended north to Springfield two years later. In the grain famine of 1640 Hartford sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-mason-connecticut">John Mason</a> to Springfield to force the sale of grain from the Native peoples, and his demand that Springfield pay a tax on ships passing Windsor pushed Springfield towards Massachusetts. Windsor stood neutral in the rivalry and finally sided with Connecticut. Tobacco, already grown by Native people when the settlers came, was exported by 1700, and Connecticut leaf became a cigar wrapper from the 1820s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 29,492 people. Windsor is governed by a nine-member council and a town manager. Its notable residents include Oliver Ellsworth, third Chief Justice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and Oliver Wolcott, both born there, and John Fitch, the steamboat inventor. A statue of John Mason stands in the town, and the home of Ellsworth is among its points of interest. From 1957 to 2006 it held a prototype naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuclear-power-plant">nuclear power plant</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Windsor, Connecticut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1636 the minister Thomas Hooker led his congregation from Massachusetts to Hartford on the Connecticut River, joining settlers at <b>Windsor</b> and Wethersfield.</blockquote><a href="/state/connecticut-colony">Connecticut Colony</a></li></ul></section>
